<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1599 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German Christmas song from the 1400s in which a rose that springs up stands for Mary, the mother of Jesus.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; three more stanzas by Friedrich Layriz (1844)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (melody printed 1599); harmonised by Michael Praetorius</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German Catholics and Protestants</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Trier, Germany (earliest manuscript)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 15th century; first printed 1599; Praetorius's harmony 1609</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous 15th-century words; tune printed 1599; harmonised by Michael Praetorius (1609))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a>&quot; (&quot;A rose has sprung up&quot;) is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> and Marian hymn of German origin, best known in English as &quot;Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming&quot; and also called &quot;A Spotless Rose&quot; and &quot;Behold a Rose of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>&quot;. It draws on the prophecies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>, which in Christian interpretation foretell the Incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and on the Tree of Jesse, the traditional image of the descent of Jesus from Jesse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a>, much loved in medieval religious art. The prophecy had featured in Christian hymns since at least the 8th century, when Cosmas the Melodist wrote of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> flowering from the root of Jesse. Because of its prophetic theme the hymn is popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a>.</p><p>The text dates from the 15th century and its author is unknown. Its earliest source is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from the Carthusian monastery of St Alban in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, now in the Trier City Library, thought to have been in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s time. It first appeared in print in the Speyer Hymnbook, printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 1599, which also holds the tune generally used; the familiar harmony was written by Michael Praetorius in 1609. A four-voice canon based on his harmony is sometimes attributed to his contemporary Melchior Vulpius. The hymn was first written with two verses. Catholics and Protestants have both sung it, the first with Mary at its centre, the second with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and it has been printed with many texts and lengths. In 1844 the hymnologist Friedrich Layriz added three stanzas, of which the first has stayed in Catholic and Protestant hymnals.</p><p>In the Nazi era many German carols were rewritten to promote Nazi ideology and to remove references to the Jewish origins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> this one was turned into &quot;Uns ist ein Licht erstanden&quot; (&quot;A light has arisen for us&quot;), a secular text of sunlight falling on the Fatherland and praise of motherhood.</p><p>Composers have returned to the melody often: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> in an organ chorale prelude of 1896, one of his Eleven Chorale Preludes, Op. 122, later orchestrated by Erich Leinsdorf; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a> in his Weihnachtsmusik of 1921, which sets it against &quot;Stille Nacht&quot;; Hugo Distler in his oratorio Die Weihnachtsgeschichte of 1933; and Jan Sandström, whose 1990 work for two a cappella choirs incorporates Praetorius's setting in the first choir. In English, Theodore Baker's &quot;Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming&quot; (1894) is sung in American hymnals, and Harriet Reynolds Krauth Spaeth wrote &quot;Behold, a rose is growing&quot;. Catherine Winkworth's translation of the first two verses, &quot;A Spotless Rose&quot;, became the text of Herbert Howells's motet of 1919. Howells said he wrote it after watching railway trucks shunting on the line below a cottage window in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, and dedicated it to his mother; its final cadence is much admired, and his fellow composer Patrick Hadley reportedly told him he would like to die to it. Philip Ledger set the same translation in 2002. The tune is also sung with &quot;A Great and Mighty Wonder&quot;, John Mason Neale's 1862 translation of a Greek hymn, and the two are sometimes confused.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A carol of the coming of Christ. A rose springs from the stem of Jesse, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> foretold, and the hymn explains that the rose is Mary, the pure maiden, who brings forth the Christ child as a small flower; in Theodore Baker's English the rose stands for the infant Christ himself. The mood is quiet and tender.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">aus einer Wurzel zart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wie uns die Alten sungen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">von Jesse kam die Art</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und hat ein Blümlein bracht</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">mitten im kalten Winter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wohl zu der halben Nacht.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das Röslein, das ich meine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">davon Isaias sagt,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">ist Maria die reine</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">die uns das Blümlein bracht.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Aus Gottes ew'gem Rat</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">hat sie ein Kind geboren</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">und blieb ein reine Magd.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">or: Welches uns selig macht.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lo, how a rose e'er blooming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>From tender stem hath sprung.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of Jesse's lineage coming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>As men of old have sung;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>It came, a flow'ret bright,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Amid the cold of winter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>When half spent was the night.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Isaiah 'twas foretold it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Rose I have in mind,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>With Mary we behold it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The virgin mother kind;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To show God's love aright,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>She bore to men a Savior,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>When half spent was the night.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous (15th century); the first two stanzas, as on German Wikisource. Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Es_ist_ein_Ros_entsprungen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: tr. Theodore Baker (1894), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Es_ist_ein_Ros_entsprungen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a>); public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="7RjAXOcTebI" data-title="Es ist ein Ros entsprungen: VOCES8, Praetorius's setting, in German"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">VOCES8, Praetorius's setting, in German</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel VOCES8. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7RjAXOcTebI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen (Michael Praetorius)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1609</span><span class="sg-chain-h">four-part harmonisation, the usual form of the hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Canon for four voices (attributed to Melchior Vulpius)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">canon on Praetorius's harmony</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming (Theodore Baker)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1894</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Spotless Rose (Catherine Winkworth)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation of the first two verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Spotless Rose (Herbert Howells)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1919</span><span class="sg-chain-h">motet on Winkworth's text</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Behold, a rose is growing (Harriet Reynolds Krauth Spaeth)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen, Op. 122 (Johannes Brahms)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1896</span><span class="sg-chain-h">organ chorale prelude on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Weihnachtsmusik (Arnold Schoenberg)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1921</span><span class="sg-chain-h">fantasy on the tune, with Stille Nacht as counterpoint</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die Weihnachtsgeschichte (Hugo Distler)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1933</span><span class="sg-chain-h">oratorio built on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Uns ist ein Licht erstanden</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Nazi-era secular rewriting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen (Jan Sandström)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting for two a cappella choirs, using Praetorius's setting in one</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Spotless Rose (Philip Ledger)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of Winkworth's text</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Great and Mighty Wonder (John Mason Neale)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1862</span><span class="sg-chain-h">another hymn sung to the same tune</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Book of Isaiah, the rod out of the stem of Jesse</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the image</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hymn of Cosmas the Melodist on the Root of Jesse</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">8th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier hymn on the same prophecy</span></li></ul></section>
